--[[
	CabinSkinConfig  (GENERATED by assets/cabins/bake.py, mirrors.py and detail_table.py; do not hand-edit the tables)
	---------------
	The first-person cabin's inner skins: one black mesh per car, baked from the car's own body
	meshes, that `Modules/FirstPersonCabin` draws in place of the car's cabin while the lens is at the
	driver's eye. The look (colour, material, how it hands over) is `CockpitConfig.Cabin`; the switch
	is `CameraConfig.FirstPerson.Cabin`.

	=== WHAT A SKIN IS ===========================================================================

	Every opaque part of the car's `Body` (not the glass, the road wheels or the steering wheel),
	merged into one mesh in the driver's SEAT space, with three things done to it:

	  - The mirrors are taken out: both door mirrors and the rear-view mirror. They are separate
	    chunks of geometry inside the vendor's meshes (often inside a door shell or the cabin tub),
	    which is why no part could simply be hidden: 30 of the 50 cars carry their door mirrors in the
	    door, and 42 carry the rear-view mirror in the tub or the body shell.
	  - Every triangle is wound to face the driver's eye (`Eye`, studs over the seat pad), so the mesh
	    shows from the seat exactly what the double-sided originals show, with no DoubleSided flag,
	    which a script cannot set. Triangles more than 105 degrees off straight ahead are dropped.
	  - It is one colour (no texture), so the inside of the shell reads as a black cabin instead of
	    the paint seen from behind.

	`Centre` is the mesh's centre in the seat's frame, so the skin sits at `seat.CFrame * Centre`.
	`Mesh` is the asset id inside the model "FP cabin skins (50 cars)", asset 76546020348436,
	uploaded 2026-10-13.

	=== REBAKING ================================================================================

	`assets/cabins/` (local, not in git) has the pipeline: `cars.json` is the roster's part dump from
	Studio (name, MeshId, Size, CFrame in the seat's frame), `eyes.json` the eye heights, `fetchall.py`
	downloads the meshes through Open Cloud, `bake.py` writes `skins.glb` and `skins.json`, and
	`preview.py` renders every skin from the eye in headless Blender. A car missing from the table
	(a new rig, a legacy body) gets the runtime fallback in `FirstPersonCabin` instead: its own parts,
	cloned black, mirrors and all. Rebake after a car's body or seat moves.

	THE MAYBACH IS LEFT OUT ON PURPOSE (`SKIP` in bake.py). Its whole shell is single-sided (the only
	car breaking the roster's double-sided rule) and its windows are painted on, so from the seat you
	see straight through it; a skin facing the eye seals it into a black box.

	=== THE DETAIL ==============================================================================

	What the skin cut out comes back, and the dash gets what the vendors never modelled. The pieces
	are one set of meshes modelled in Blender (`assets/cabins/details_blend.py`, uploaded 2026-10-14
	as the model "FP cabin details", asset 113555204726926), placed per car by `anchors.py`, which
	casts rays at the skin and the car's glass:

	  Cluster  the gauges, where the eye looks through the top half of the wheel, facing the eye
	  Screen   the radio, standing on the dash top just past its near edge, on the centreline
	  Row      a vent, the hazard switch and a vent, on the centre stack under the dash top
	  Vent     one more vent at the driver's end of the dash (about half the cars have room)
	  Mirror   the rear-view mirror where the car's own was, on a stalk to the roof or the glass

	Every `At` is a frame in the SEAT's frame with its Z toward the driver (a part's Back face), so
	a piece's part sits at `seat.CFrame * At * CFrame.new(DetailMeshes[name].Centre * Scale)`. `Mount`
	is the point (seat frame) the stalk runs to. `Scale` sizes the piece: the meshes are 1 stud wide
	where a width means anything. `DetailMeshes` is each mesh's box in the piece's own frame, which is
	where Roblox puts a MeshPart's origin. (The asset also holds a pair of door-mirror pieces of our
	own, retired the same day: Dylan preferred the cars' own, below.)

	THE DOOR MIRRORS ARE THE CARS' OWN (`Mirrors`). `assets/cabins/mirrors.py` takes exactly the
	chunks the skin cut for the door mirrors (door or body shell, trim, chrome, lenses), UVs and all,
	one mesh per car, side and source part (model "FP cabin door mirrors (48 cars)", asset
	72362103571495). Each is drawn as the live part it came from (`Part`, a path under the car):
	its TextureID, colour and material, so the mirror is the car's paint and trim exactly. `Centre`
	is the mesh's centre in the seat's frame and the mesh is already turned for the importer, so the
	part sits at `seat.CFrame * CFrame.new(Centre)`. The Roadster is a convertible with none.

	The previews to check a re-run against are `assets/cabins/preview_details.py` (every car from
	the eye, in headless Blender) and `sheet.py` (a contact sheet of them).
]]

local CabinSkinConfig = {}

export type Skin = { Mesh: number, Centre: Vector3, Eye: number }

export type Piece = { At: CFrame, Scale: number?, Mount: Vector3? }
export type Detail = {
	Cluster: Piece?,
	Screen: Piece?,
	Row: Piece?,
	Vent: Piece?,
	Mirror: Piece?,
}
export type DetailMesh = { Mesh: number, Centre: Vector3, Size: Vector3 }
export type CarMirror = { Part: string, Mesh: number, Centre: Vector3 }

